Subject: [overlay 0038] VR: the aim ray drives the weapon / inventory wheel

In a VR world frame nothing ever called CL_Wheel_Input — the Sense sticks go to the
snap-turn snapshot and CL_AdjustAngles (which carries the mouse/touch/stick wheel
producers) is never reached — so the wheel opened, drew, holstered the gun and was
inert: position stayed (0,0), distance 0, selected -1, and the release confirmed
nothing.  This adds the fourth cursor producer, entirely inside the client (the wheel
never touches game code): CL_iOS_VRWheelCursorNDC takes the aim ray as normalised HUD
quad coordinates and converts them into the wheel's own pixel space, against geometry
CAPTURED AT DRAW TIME because on visionOS r_config is only the HUD sub-rect inside the
compositor's UI bracket.  Plus CL_iOS_WheelOpen / CL_iOS_WheelSelected (the shell's
crosshair-visibility, flick-suppression and haptic reads) and CL_iOS_WheelDump
(WHEELNOW, the suite's assertion channel).  patch -p1 -d vendor/q2repro < this


--- a/src/client/wheel.c
+++ b/src/client/wheel.c
@@ -24,6 +24,18 @@
 // wheel appears under the finger and the touch cursor's origin is the button. -1 = default.
 static vec2_t cl_ios_wheel_anchor = { -1.f, -1.f };
 void CL_iOS_SetWheelAnchor(float ux, float uy) { cl_ios_wheel_anchor[0] = ux; cl_ios_wheel_anchor[1] = uy; }
+
+// [iOS overlay 0038] VR: the aim ray drives the wheel cursor, and the geometry it is converted
+// against is captured HERE, at draw time, rather than read at input time.
+//
+// Why: on visionOS the 2D stream is laid out for a widescreen SUB-RECT of the eye texture, and
+// r_config is switched to that sub-rect only inside the compositor's UI bracket. Read from the
+// input path — which runs outside the bracket — scr.hud_width/height/scale are the EYE's shape,
+// not the quad's, and a cursor converted against them would land somewhere the player is not
+// looking. Captured in CL_Wheel_Draw the mapping is exact by construction: the pixels the ray is
+// converted into are the pixels that were drawn.
+static float cl_ios_wheel_geo[5];   // half_w, half_h, centre_x, centre_y, wheel_size (REAL px)
+static int   cl_ios_wheel_geo_ok;
 
